{:edb/db-before-t 2542 :edb/db-after-t 2543 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535855 50 #inst "2026-10-09T09:03:28.661Z" 13194139535855 true] [17592186087805 1020 #edb/ref 17592186370521 13194139535855 true] [17592186087805 1020 #edb/ref 17592186370549 13194139535855 true] [17592186087805 1020 #edb/ref 17592186370556 13194139535855 true] [17592186087806 1020 #edb/ref 17592186370514 13194139535855 true] [17592186087806 1020 #edb/ref 17592186370563 13194139535855 true] [17592186087807 1020 #edb/ref 17592186370528 13194139535855 true] [17592186087807 1020 #edb/ref 17592186370535 13194139535855 true] [17592186087807 1020 #edb/ref 17592186370542 13194139535855 true] [17592186087973 1100 "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1-4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$" 13194139535855 true] [17592186087973 1100 "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$2\\frac{dy}{dx} - 3\\cos x = 1 - 4y\\sin x$" 13194139535855 false] [17592186087973 1145 "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =1-4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{\\frac{1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}.$" 13194139535855 true] [17592186087973 1145 "The standard form is $y^{\\prime}+P(x)y=Q(x)$. Move the $y$ term to the left and the cosine term to the right: $2y^{\\prime}+4y\\sin x=1+3\\cos x$. Dividing every term by $2$ gives $y^{\\prime}+(2\\sin x)y=\\frac{1+3\\cos x}{2}$." 13194139535855 false] [17592186088362 1145 "The equation $y^{\\prime}+2xy=6x^2$ is first-order and linear: $P(x)=2x$ and $Q(x)=6x^2$. The other choices contain $(y^{\\prime})^2$, a second derivative, $y^2$, or the product $yy^{\\prime}$, each of which violates first-order linear form." 13194139535855 false] [17592186088362 1145 "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that the option\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy=6{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nis already is written in standard form with  $P(x)=2x$ and $Q(x)=6{x}^{2}.$ Since the equation is written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations cannot be written in the standard form, and therefore they are not first-order linear ODEs." 13194139535855 true] [17592186370514 1017 #edb/ref 17592186370515 13194139535855 true] [17592186370514 1089 "q-103567" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370514 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535855 true] [17592186370514 1100 "Which of the following is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370514 1113 #uuid "420cf089-e2ca-55d0-b4d5-3e91f54aadf7" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370514 1145 "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to write $\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x{e}^{2x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{y}$ in the standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x{e}^{2x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x{e}^{2x}y & =y\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x{e}^{2x}y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is in standard form with  $P(x)=-x{e}^{2x}$ and $Q(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Since the equation is written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations cannot be written in the standard form, and therefore they are not first-order linear ODEs." 13194139535855 true] [17592186370515 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370516 13194139535855 true] [17592186370515 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370517 13194139535855 true] [17592186370515 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370518 13194139535855 true] [17592186370515 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370519 13194139535855 true] [17592186370515 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370520 13194139535855 true] [17592186370515 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535855 true] [17592186370515 1041 "selection" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370515 1104 #edb/ref 17592186370516 13194139535855 true] [17592186370515 1110 #uuid "835b469b-e1d0-52d7-9659-86f89681c74e" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370516 1034 "\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x{e}^{2x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{y}" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370516 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535855 true] [17592186370516 1111 #uuid "5e1a28a4-3e38-5072-b4a2-dc4c03f25705" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370517 1034 "\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x{e}^{2x}y=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{y}" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370517 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535855 true] [17592186370517 1111 #uuid "eb8264bc-dc17-5abc-aa89-7ad75fe0b602" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370518 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x{e}^{y}={x}^{3}" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370518 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535855 true] [17592186370518 1111 #uuid "99662701-943b-5fc4-890b-9bf5468f736b" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370519 1034 "y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x{e}^{x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{y}" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370519 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535855 true] [17592186370519 1111 #uuid "9d5448e4-1987-57d3-9205-e1a9b2118b71" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370520 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-y{e}^{2x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{y}" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370520 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535855 true] [17592186370520 1111 #uuid "0cf5376d-0615-5f31-b249-60808562f1c8" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370521 1017 #edb/ref 17592186370522 13194139535855 true] [17592186370521 1089 "q-16396" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370521 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535855 true] [17592186370521 1100 "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\ny\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}y={y}^{2}x\\,,\\,y\\ne 0\\,.\n$$" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370521 1113 #uuid "ec00018b-fca6-56bf-9fcb-4fbfc8967696" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370521 1145 "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\\,,\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}y & ={y}^{2}x \\\\ y\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}y-{y}^{2}x & =0 \\\\ y(\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}-yx) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}-yx & =0 \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}+\\,{{(-x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{-{x}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)} \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}-xy & =-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=-x$ and $Q(x)=-{x}^{2}.$" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370522 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370523 13194139535855 true] [17592186370522 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370524 13194139535855 true] [17592186370522 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370525 13194139535855 true] [17592186370522 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370526 13194139535855 true] [17592186370522 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370527 13194139535855 true] [17592186370522 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535855 true] [17592186370522 1041 "selection" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370522 1104 #edb/ref 17592186370526 13194139535855 true] [17592186370522 1110 #uuid "9b72a3fb-aa2b-5aee-9f67-f74386aea68a" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370523 1034 "\\frac{dy}{dx}+\\frac{1-{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{2}}y=1" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370523 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535855 true] [17592186370523 1111 #uuid "202f2d3b-d782-54e0-9d50-20324448a29f" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370524 1034 "\\frac{dy}{dx}-y=-2x" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370524 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535855 true] [17592186370524 1111 #uuid "d3034924-4b7f-5d04-87e2-8d20ab54853a" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370525 1034 "\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}y=x" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370525 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535855 true] [17592186370525 1111 #uuid "37b9f416-0d33-5022-abbb-fb8765da84ed" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370526 1034 "\\frac{dy}{dx}-xy=-{x}^{2}" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370526 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535855 true] [17592186370526 1111 #uuid "b1ea6302-298f-5852-9619-eee9d8bfc112" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370527 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-{x}^{2}" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370527 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535855 true] [17592186370527 1111 #uuid "8d658bfb-edef-57fe-88cb-aa58e6627513" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370528 1017 #edb/ref 17592186370529 13194139535855 true] [17592186370528 1089 "q-331724" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370528 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535855 true] [17592186370528 1100 "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y={x}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=-3y$\n- ${y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=0$" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370528 1113 #uuid "47c2e09f-c0f7-56b8-b5a4-7e98b72cdc6c" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370528 1145 "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)={x}^{2}$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=-3y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y & =-3y \\\\ {y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y+3y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+(2{x}^{3}+3)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370529 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370530 13194139535855 true] [17592186370529 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370531 13194139535855 true] [17592186370529 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370532 13194139535855 true] [17592186370529 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370533 13194139535855 true] [17592186370529 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370534 13194139535855 true] [17592186370529 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535855 true] [17592186370529 1041 "selection" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370529 1104 #edb/ref 17592186370531 13194139535855 true] [17592186370529 1110 #uuid "24fc08da-8471-5a09-8390-f7258f4ee14e" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370530 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370530 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535855 true] [17592186370530 1111 #uuid "34d35e32-b10c-534a-be15-2cf90467de39" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370531 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370531 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535855 true] [17592186370531 1111 #uuid "6a762ec9-6315-51df-895e-77ae8fba1514" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370532 1034 "I only" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370532 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535855 true] [17592186370532 1111 #uuid "4a03aed5-df62-5eb0-b0a0-7758e463580e" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370533 1034 "III only" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370533 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535855 true] [17592186370533 1111 #uuid "70d24f13-cf29-5e13-becd-f507c2aada4f" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370534 1034 "II only" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370534 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535855 true] [17592186370534 1111 #uuid "99b06fcb-4874-596f-8545-e6dd462e09f4" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370535 1017 #edb/ref 17592186370536 13194139535855 true] [17592186370535 1089 "q-331727" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370535 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535855 true] [17592186370535 1100 "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=-3y$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=x$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0$" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370535 1113 #uuid "4fc87950-d591-5333-8bfb-dd5aa9b78b7b" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370535 1145 "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=-3y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y & =-3y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y+3y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{2}+3)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=x.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=x$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370536 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370537 13194139535855 true] [17592186370536 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370538 13194139535855 true] [17592186370536 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370539 13194139535855 true] [17592186370536 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370540 13194139535855 true] [17592186370536 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370541 13194139535855 true] [17592186370536 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535855 true] [17592186370536 1041 "selection" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370536 1104 #edb/ref 17592186370538 13194139535855 true] [17592186370536 1110 #uuid "4c1850d7-1a6d-59a6-b56e-3444e0564b36" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370537 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370537 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535855 true] [17592186370537 1111 #uuid "c4ac63c7-9e16-5896-9b00-031871696efb" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370538 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370538 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535855 true] [17592186370538 1111 #uuid "5cfe0a84-8ced-5f34-bf7b-0743a8aa0a3d" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370539 1034 "I only" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370539 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535855 true] [17592186370539 1111 #uuid "cef4c664-c04b-5a11-9961-c6998c3a2a49" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370540 1034 "III only" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370540 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535855 true] [17592186370540 1111 #uuid "ec0f3bd0-c71a-50d3-9758-0c003ccdb155" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370541 1034 "II only" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370541 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535855 true] [17592186370541 1111 #uuid "4287d5b0-0643-57c7-87c5-fe38b332f22f" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370542 1017 #edb/ref 17592186370543 13194139535855 true] [17592186370542 1089 "q-331739" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370542 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535855 true] [17592186370542 1100 "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y={x}^{3}$\n- ${y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y=y$\n- ${y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y=0$" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370542 1113 #uuid "8ae95313-5ad6-52d8-8e38-94f4839c14a8" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370542 1145 "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y={x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)={x}^{3}$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-({x}^{2}+1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370543 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370544 13194139535855 true] [17592186370543 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370545 13194139535855 true] [17592186370543 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370546 13194139535855 true] [17592186370543 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370547 13194139535855 true] [17592186370543 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370548 13194139535855 true] [17592186370543 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535855 true] [17592186370543 1041 "selection" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370543 1104 #edb/ref 17592186370545 13194139535855 true] [17592186370543 1110 #uuid "05abac35-1617-51ec-bfb5-85e6776904f3" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370544 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370544 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535855 true] [17592186370544 1111 #uuid "1a40f4f8-d24a-51ed-95ee-387b8987a252" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370545 1034 "I only" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370545 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535855 true] [17592186370545 1111 #uuid "0abab218-2c14-51f4-8d8e-30f77e1e56ba" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370546 1034 "III only" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370546 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535855 true] [17592186370546 1111 #uuid "db5ce2a9-0906-5a68-9103-2db968c517f8" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370547 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370547 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535855 true] [17592186370547 1111 #uuid "f5129718-2a0e-538f-aaa7-e78f1c18d852" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370548 1034 "II only" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370548 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535855 true] [17592186370548 1111 #uuid "cf481d94-42ca-53d5-8b86-ee0372b2a33f" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370549 1017 #edb/ref 17592186370550 13194139535855 true] [17592186370549 1089 "q-79697" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370549 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535855 true] [17592186370549 1100 "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x+y}{x}\\,.\n$$" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370549 1113 #uuid "796eac59-5d6b-548a-bcd1-69a1ed0d770d" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370549 1145 "A first order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\\,,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ do not depend on $y.$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x+y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{2}(\\frac{x+y}{x}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x(x+y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{2}+xy \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,+{{(-x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{{x}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=-x$ and $Q(x)={x}^{2}.$" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370550 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370551 13194139535855 true] [17592186370550 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370552 13194139535855 true] [17592186370550 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370553 13194139535855 true] [17592186370550 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370554 13194139535855 true] [17592186370550 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370555 13194139535855 true] [17592186370550 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535855 true] [17592186370550 1041 "selection" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370550 1104 #edb/ref 17592186370554 13194139535855 true] [17592186370550 1110 #uuid "0fb74fb1-d9f0-5ca2-b97d-af491c2e5634" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370551 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-y=-{x}^{2}" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370551 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535855 true] [17592186370551 1111 #uuid "13b8bb7e-eab1-53c6-8bfc-5c37495edfbc" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370552 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy=2x" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370552 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535855 true] [17592186370552 1111 #uuid "a2ff3adc-1903-597f-a75f-bac879c2c06a" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370553 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(1+x)y=0" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370553 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535855 true] [17592186370553 1111 #uuid "90c54b70-9041-54f5-a98f-4ff97b31361c" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370554 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy={x}^{2}" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370554 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535855 true] [17592186370554 1111 #uuid "1e48ae81-f48a-5424-ba13-c38e5ba9e359" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370555 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy=\\frac{1}{x}" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370555 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535855 true] [17592186370555 1111 #uuid "89aa8264-9cf4-590d-aac9-fc9a329645ac" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370556 1017 #edb/ref 17592186370557 13194139535855 true] [17592186370556 1089 "q-79699" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370556 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535855 true] [17592186370556 1100 "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n5{y}^{2}-y{e}^{-x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y,\\,y\\ne 0.\n$$" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370556 1113 #uuid "fd03ddd9-f328-514d-b88e-e72a03336608" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370556 1145 "A first order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\\,,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ do not depend on $y.$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}^{2}-y{e}^{-x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ -y{e}^{-x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+5{y}^{2}-y & =0 \\\\ -y({e}^{-x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5y+1) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{-x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5y+1 & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}({e}^{-x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5y+1) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5y{e}^{x}+{e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,+{{(-5{e}^{x})}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{-{e}^{x}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5{e}^{x}y & =-{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=-5{e}^{x}$ and $Q(x)=-{e}^{x}.$" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370557 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370558 13194139535855 true] [17592186370557 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370559 13194139535855 true] [17592186370557 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370560 13194139535855 true] [17592186370557 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370561 13194139535855 true] [17592186370557 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370562 13194139535855 true] [17592186370557 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535855 true] [17592186370557 1041 "selection" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370557 1104 #edb/ref 17592186370560 13194139535855 true] [17592186370557 1110 #uuid "fb0dacc1-ae55-5143-a1dc-f1223c122e00" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370558 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,-5{e}^{x}y=x" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370558 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535855 true] [17592186370558 1111 #uuid "6640317a-3cb8-5bea-83f0-17292460461f" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370559 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5xy=-{e}^{-x}" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370559 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535855 true] [17592186370559 1111 #uuid "57d50d0b-cc09-510e-b593-6ba9ccc3ac07" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370560 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,-5{e}^{x}y=-{e}^{x}" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370560 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535855 true] [17592186370560 1111 #uuid "7f9d4383-844f-5866-ac43-943f2bcff600" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370561 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,+5y=-{e}^{x}" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370561 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535855 true] [17592186370561 1111 #uuid "0899a31f-35a4-5aa2-8d5b-c6f37a79503e" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370562 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,+5{e}^{-x}y={e}^{-x}" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370562 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535855 true] [17592186370562 1111 #uuid "d8f8d131-4f75-57e5-948c-c600eda34afd" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370563 1017 #edb/ref 17592186370564 13194139535855 true] [17592186370563 1089 "q-79753" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370563 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535855 true] [17592186370563 1100 "Which of the following is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370563 1113 #uuid "56009a7b-7322-5f2a-a18b-b5d02247377f" 13194139535855 true] [17592186370563 1145 "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that the option\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(\\sqrt{x})y=x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\n$$\n\nis already is written in st